    "body": "AI for professors is most useful on the work that piles up: reading student drafts, writing grant proposals, and checking a manuscript before it goes out. AI for Research gives academics a Reviewer that scores a draft at the right level, a Proposal Studio for grant formats, claim and citation checks for manuscripts, and projects shared with students and co-authors.\n\n## Three jobs you can do here\n\n### 1. Give students structured feedback on drafts\n\nWhere your institution allows it, upload a student's chapter to the **Reviewer**, choose the document type (thesis, proposal or manuscript) and the level (undergraduate, honours, master's or PhD). You get a score out of 10 for each criterion and major and minor comments, each quoting the passage with a suggested fix. Use it as a first read that frees your own reading for the argument. Standard costs 10 credits per 10,000 words.\n\n### 2. Draft grant proposals in your funder's format\n\n**Proposal Studio** writes a grant proposal in a generic, NIH, NSF, ERC or Wellcome shape. Paste the call, its criteria and limits, and your idea. A whole draft costs 15 credits; one section (aims, significance, approach, budget justification) costs 3. It also drafts ethics applications (IRB, UK REC, HREC) and data management plans.\n\n### 3. Check a manuscript before submission\n\nRun **Claim check** on key paragraphs (2 credits): each claim is labeled supported, partly supported, not supported or no evidence, with the papers. Run the **citation check** on the reference list (free): it confirms each reference exists and flags retractions, corrections, expressions of concern and wrong years or authors. Then format to the journal: 10,000+ citation styles, Word export, and LaTeX export on paid plans.\n\n## How it works, step by step\n\n1. **Create a project** per student, grant or paper. Invite students as Can edit on their own project, co-supervisors as Can comment.\n2. **Review a draft.** In the Reviewer, upload the file (PDF or Word), pick My documents, or paste text. Choose type, level and checks, and describe the formatting rules if you want them checked.\n3. **Read the result** beside the draft: the scorecard, then major comments, then minor. Copy the comments you agree with into your own feedback.\n4. **Start a grant.** In Proposal Studio choose Grant proposal and the funder, paste the call, and run one section or the whole proposal. Open it in the editor.\n5. **Ground the background.** Run a Deep report (15 credits) on the gap the grant addresses, save the cited papers, and cite them in the funder's style.\n6. **Check before submitting.** Claim check on the significance section, citation check on the reference list, export to Word or LaTeX.\n\n## The workspaces you will use\n\n| Workspace | What you do with it | Credits |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| [Reviewer](/features/ai-peer-review) | Feedback on student drafts and your own manuscripts | 10 per 10,000 words (High quality 30, Pro) |\n| [Proposal Studio](/features/proposal-studio) | Grant proposals, ethics applications, data management plans | Section 3, whole 15 |\n| [Citation checker](/features/citation-checker) | Claim check; reference check | 2; 0 |\n| [Deep Research](/features/deep-research) | Background and literature for a grant or paper | 1, 15, 45 (paid) |\n| [Projects](/features/projects) | Shared folders for students and co-authors | 0 |\n| [Word and LaTeX export](/features/word-latex-export) | Journal-ready files | 0 (LaTeX on paid plans) |\n| [Research slides](/features/research-slides) | A talk or lecture deck from a paper or report | 10 |\n\n## Which plan fits, and a month worked out\n\nMost academics fit **Plus** ($12 a month, 750 credits, 10 projects with 3 people each) or **Pro** ($29 a month, 1,500 credits, unlimited projects with 10 people each, High quality reviews). For a lab, department or writing group, **Team** gives every member Pro, 2,000 credits a seat a month pooled, shared projects and Library, from 3 seats on one invoice, arranged through sales.\n\n**Example month (invented, on Pro):** a lecturer supervising four master's students, writing an NIH application and revising a paper.\n\n| Job | How many | Credits |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Reviewer, Standard, four 15,000-word master's drafts | 4 × 2 units | 80 |\n| Reviewer, Standard, two 8,000-word undergraduate dissertations | 2 × 1 unit | 20 |\n| NIH grant proposal, whole draft | 1 | 15 |\n| Grant sections rewritten | 6 | 18 |\n| Deep reports | 3 | 45 |\n| Detailed report | 1 | 45 |\n| Summary answers | 20 | 20 |\n| Editor checks on the paper (claim check, proofread, tone) | 10 | 20 |\n| Lecture slide deck | 1 | 10 |\n| Citation check, citations, exports, projects | any | 0 |\n| **Total** | | **273 of 1,500** |\n\nThe same month fits on Plus; Pro is for the 10-person projects and High quality reviews.\n\n## AI-use rules that matter to you\n\n- **Peer review.** NIH prohibits reviewers from using generative AI to analyze or critique applications, and uploading application content breaches confidentiality [1]. NSF extends reviewers' confidentiality duty to AI tools [2]. Never upload material you are reviewing.\n- **Grant applications.** NIH does not treat applications substantially developed by AI as the applicant's original ideas [3]. NSF says proposers are responsible for AI-assisted content and encourages them to say how AI was used [2].\n- **Papers.** ICMJE asks journals to require disclosure of AI use, and says chatbots cannot be authors and humans are responsible for all content [4].\n- **Student work.** Follow your institution's rules on processing student work with AI, and tell students when you use it.\n\nRules change: check the funder's or journal's current page before each submission. For a lab policy built on these rules, see [AI tools for research labs](/blog/ai-tools-for-research-labs).\n\n## What it does not do\n\n- It does not grade. Scores are feedback, not marks, and the Reviewer can miss problems an expert sees.\n- It does not submit to funders or journals, or fill their online forms.\n- It does not search Google Scholar, Scopus or Web of Science.\n- Team is not self-serve: it is set up through sales.\n\n## Who else it is for\n\nYour students may want [AI for a master's thesis](/for/masters) or [AI tools for PhD students](/for/phd). Clinical academics, see [AI for medical research](/for/medicine).\n\n## Learn more\n\n- [AI tools for research labs](/blog/ai-tools-for-research-labs)\n- [How to respond to reviewer comments](/blog/how-to-respond-to-reviewer-comments)\n- [AI grant writing](/blog/ai-grant-writing)\n- [AI in academic research: policies](/blog/ai-in-academic-research-policies)\n\n## Sources\n\n1. National Institutes of Health. NOT-OD-23-149: The use of generative artificial intelligence technologies is prohibited for the NIH peer review process (23 June 2023). https://grants.nih.gov/grants/guide/notice-files/NOT-OD-23-149.html\n2. National Science Foundation. Notice to research community: Use of generative artificial intelligence technology in the NSF merit review process (14 December 2023). https://www.nsf.gov/policies/ai/merit-review\n3. National Institutes of Health. NOT-OD-25-132: Supporting fairness and originality in NIH research applications (17 July 2025). https://grants.nih.gov/grants/guide/notice-files/NOT-OD-25-132.html\n4. International Committee of Medical Journal Editors. Recommendations: AI use by authors. https://www.icmje.org/recommendations/browse/artificial-intelligence/ai-use-by-authors.html"
